VB.NET notes (binding memo: docs/fairness-audit.md, "Language admission memos").

k-nucleotide: C# uses the .NET 9 Dictionary.GetAlternateLookup<ReadOnlySpan<char>>() zero-allocation
probe. VB cannot express it. Verified with the SDK image mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:9.0 (VB 17.x
compiler) on:

    Dim lookup = map.GetAlternateLookup(Of ReadOnlySpan(Of Char))()
    Dim kmer As ReadOnlySpan(Of Char) = s.AsSpan(0, 4)

Both lines fail with:

    error BC30668: 'ReadOnlySpan(Of Char)' is obsolete: 'Types with embedded references are not
    supported in this version of your compiler.'

So the VB program allocates one String per probe (s.Substring), like Java, Kotlin and Go.

Allocation lint (GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread at exit, n1 vs n2): every allocation-free
benchmark shows only the size-proportional setup buffers, except sha256, which allocates 712 bytes
per hash (w, b and h arrays inside the timed loop). These are the same three allocations per hash as the C#
program (byte sizes differ because the VB words are Long), kept for the like-for-like comparison.

Indexing note: Option Strict On (binding for every VB port) is the reason CInt appears on Long
indices: it forbids Long array indices, so the loops that C# runs over a long
counter (blur, dijkstra, k-means, gemm, sort-search, vm) index through CInt(...), a checked narrowing.
